WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.238
America's public health workforce is collapsing.

2
00:00:03.594 --> 00:00:07.078
Why are the desks empty when we spend so much on health care?

3
00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:12.454
It's not a lack of graduates. It's failing to treat health spending as economic infrastructure.

4
00:00:13.034 --> 00:00:18.598
Take a local health department trying to retain one trained epidemiologist during a non-emergency year.

5
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:23.526
Right now, one third of state and local public health workers are considering leaving.

6
00:00:23.882 --> 00:00:29.638
In vital specialties like epidemiology and laboratory science, vacancy rates hit 50%.

7
00:00:30.122 --> 00:00:32.646
It's driven by a boom and bust funding cycle.

8
00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:36.006
During a crisis, emergency money violently spikes.

9
00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:37.766
Then immediately vanishes.

10
00:00:38.186 --> 00:00:43.814
Without predictable funding, that department can't offer competitive salaries. This triggers a vicious cycle.

11
00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:46.470
Funding flatlines, but the workload doubles.

12
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:50.790
Overworked staff burn out and leave, so chronic diseases go unmanaged.

13
00:00:51.146 --> 00:00:55.622
When the health department is empty, those patients are diverted to hospital emergency rooms.

14
00:00:55.882 --> 00:00:59.878
This racks up hundreds of thousands of dollars in excess residual costs.

15
00:01:00.202 --> 00:01:07.590
Mathematically, the steady cost of maintaining permanent standing capacity is a fraction of funding emergency hospital responses later.

16
00:01:08.042 --> 00:01:13.542
So keeping that single trained epidemiologist on staff isn't just a basic administrative expense.

17
00:01:13.802 --> 00:01:19.430
It is the essential economic infrastructure that permanently protects the community's budget from the next crisis.

18
00:01:19.914 --> 00:01:20.998
Human in purpose.

19
00:01:21.258 --> 00:01:22.374
Systemic in method.

20
00:01:22.794 --> 00:01:23.878
Protective in outcome.
